ated board, hoping opponent folds weaker hands (like K-high, Q-high, or missed draws).\n\nOpponent may believe you have:\n- 4-x for a straight,\n- A-T or A-3 for two pair,\n- Or a heart flush.\n\nEven though you don’t, the story you're telling (aggression earlier, then check on turn, bet on river) can be believable.\n\nReason:  \nDespite having no pair, the board is very coordinated and many players will fear straights and flushes. A small 100-chip bet is low risk, high reward — it can fold out worse hands (like K-high, Q-high, J-high, or missed draws). Given that you were aggressive earlier, this continuation bet can look consistent with a strong range. Folding loses for sure; checking may lead to free showdown where you lose. A small bluff maximizes expected value here.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
