rong hand (like high pocket pairs or AK) or a bluff with a marginal hand. However, going all-in immediately as the first action often indicates strength, especially in early positions or short-stacked situations.  \n- Given that we both started with 20,000 chips and I now have 19,900, my opponent must have gone all-in for 20,000 (since I haven’t bet yet). That means committing my remaining 19,900 to call would put me at risk of elimination if I lose.  \n- The pot would be 20,000 (opponent’s all-in) + 19,900 (my call) = 39,900. I’d need better than 50% equity to justify this call against an unknown range, but pocket deuces are typically a coin flip against a random hand and a dog against any decent starting hand like TT+, AK, AQ.  \n- Since my opponent acted first and went all-in immediately, they likely have a strong range. In most strategic contexts, players don’t go all-in pre-flop with weak hands unless desperate or bluffing frequently — but without reads on aggression tendencies, we assume rational play.\n\nReason:  \nCalling with 22 against an immediate all-in from an opponent who likely has a strong range is too risky. Although 22 can win roughly 30–40% of the time against many hands, it's dominated by any higher pair and has no immediate draw potential. Folding preserves 19,900 chips for better opportunities in future hands, especially since I’m already behind cumulatively and need to conserve resources.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
